{"id": "040bff45-c276-414c-9221-15e532a5a528", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:54:46.313464", "source": "user", "parent_id": "44ef9a62-eddb-4b2b-929a-7bd224c72fc1", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Namaste. This is the opening round of an inspect-only review for a design-only deliverable, so please do not yet draft the final experiment design. Begin by sharing what you actually see, then propose how you would proceed, and finally flag the consequential assumptions and uncertainties. Specifically I want the following three items in this single response, and nothing more: (1) Input and repository inspection. List the contents of /workspace at the top level, show the full text of track.md verbatim, and report whether any of TorchOpt, TorchViz, or torch source trees are locally available; if not, state that explicitly. Do not assume any external network access and do not attempt to install packages. (2) Proposed approach for the experiment design. Given the question — whether fused operations inside the Adam optimizer (TorchOpt-style) yield a less complex and more interpretable gradient/compute graph than the graph produced via TorchViz for an unfused baseline — sketch how you would define the two conditions, the reference model and inputs, the metric set for graph complexity and interpretability (for example node count, edge count, operator diversity, graph depth, subgraph size per conceptual block such as Adam step vs parameter update), the controls (fixed model seed, identical hyperparameters, identical forward/backward boundary, identical device), and the analysis plan (paired comparison across layers/seeds, effect sizes, qualitative operator-level walkthrough). Justify each choice briefly. (3) Consequential assumptions and uncertainties. Enumerate what you are assuming about TorchOpt's fused-Adam behavior, about what TorchViz actually renders (forward graph, backward graph, or both), about how 'complexity' and 'interpretability' will be operationalized, about feasibility given that the bubblewrap runtime has no network, no preinstalled ML stack, and is design-only, and about any source-supported setup detail you would need clarified before finalizing the design. Keep the response compact and evidence-grounded. No claims of running code, no fabricated TorchOpt internals, and no final deliverable yet — we will iterate.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"plan\", \"approach_approved\": false, \"public_task\": {\"question\": \"Does fusing operations within the Adam optimizer (as done in TorchOpt) result in a less complex and more interpretable gradient graph compared to the output from TorchViz?\", \"context\": \"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\", \"environment\": {\"runtime\": \"bubblewrap\", \"network\": \"disabled\", \"repository\": \"/workspace\", \"python\": \"python3\", \"software_environment\": \"system Python; project dependencies not preinstalled\", \"datasets\": [], \"gpu_enabled\": false, \"cpu_affinity_count\": 24, \"note\": \"Successful exploratory commands are not proof of a completed experiment.\"}, \"researcher_topics\": [], \"question_budget\": 6, \"actor_step_budget\": 60, \"continue_after_initial_plan\": false}}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 1}
