{"id": "0efbad04-9411-48ed-9613-1378169ea062", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T17:56:59.356328", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "5d328d82-3ffc-4cb5-abd6-642e75e8ad6c", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-267c2a91-1103-4c31-87d5-f8fc91634077", "tool_call": {"id": "call-267c2a91-1103-4c31-87d5-f8fc91634077", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Final design: 4 conditions, primary metrics, paired analysis\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"Final experiment design — design-only deliverable. No code executed; no experiment claimed to have run.\\n\\n================================================================\\nTitle: Fused Adam (TorchOpt) vs Unfused Adam — Gradient/Compute Graph Complexity and Interpretability\\n================================================================\\n\\n1. Research question (as supplied, restated)\\n   Does fusing operations within the Adam optimizer (as done in TorchOpt) result in a less complex and more interpretable gradient graph compared to the output from TorchViz?\\n\\n2. Conditions (four, paired on seed and parameter snapshot)\\n   C0   Baseline (PyTorch)        : `torch.optim.Adam(params, lr, betas, eps, weight_decay)` — one `step()`.\\n   C1   TorchOpt — fused          : `torchopt.Adam(params, lr, betas, eps, weight_decay)` — one `step()`. Treated as the primary TorchOpt mirror.\\n   C2   TorchOpt — fused / meta   : `torchopt.MetaAdam(params, lr, betas, eps, weight_decay)` — one `step()`. Or whichever current TorchOpt alias wraps the differentiable fused-meta variant; the alias is a protocol-level placeholder [P-1].\\n   C_ped Pedagogical unfused      : A from-scratch re-implementation of the Adam update written as plain PyTorch elementwise ops (no fused call), reproducing `(m, v, m_hat, v_hat, theta)` in the canonical order. Acts as the conceptual unfused floor independent of framework defaults.\\n\\n   Rationale for the four-way design: the question conflates two readings of “fusing operations within Adam” (state-fused step kernels for plain Adam, and fused-meta-differentiable stepping). Including both is the only way to avoid silently preferring one. C_ped exists so the interpretability comparison is not silently `torch.optim.Adam defaults` vs TorchOpt — it is `conceptually unfused` vs `fused`.\\n\\n3. Reference model and inputs (locked)\\n   - Model: 3-layer MLP, hidden size 64, activations = ReLU, final linear head. Parameter count kept small (~20k) so the rendered DOT graph is human-inspectable but non-trivial. [Protocol placeholder: dimensions can be re-pinned at execution time without changing metric definitions.]\\n   - Inputs: fixed synthetic regression batch `x ∈ ℝ^{B×D}` with `B=32`, `D=16`, `y = W* x + ε`; seed pinned.\\n   - Loss: MSE between model output and `y`. One forward, one `loss.backward()`, one optimizer step — no extra training iteration.\\n   - Device: CPU only. Dtype: float32. No CUDA, no AMP.\\n\\n4. Graph acquisition (the same call shape across all four conditions)\\n   - After the optimizer step, call:\\n        `dot = torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters()))   # kwarg shape [P-2]`\\n   - This call is identical byte-for-byte in C0, C1, C2, and C_ped. Differs only in the optimizer used for the step.\\n   - The rendered graph is interpreted as the full autograd graph visible to `make_dot` under this call: forward edges + backward edges terminating at parameter leaves. Per researcher resolution (b) and (c), the “gradient graph” is this full graph; the optimizer-step subgraph is the primary slice within it.\\n\\n5. Metrics (operationalized so a future executor can implement mechanically)\\n\\n   Notation. Let `G = (N, E)` be the rendered graph and write `sub(s)` for the optimizer-step subgraph (Section 5.3). All counts are computed from `dot.body` / `dot.edges()` after parsing with `pydot` or `graphviz.Source`’s string output [P-3]. Each metric is computed per-seed per-condition; later aggregated.\\n\\n   5.1 Primary metrics (optimizer-step subgraph only)\\n       M1  `|N_sub|`            — number of nodes inside `sub(s)`.\\n       M2  `op_diversity_sub`   — number of distinct op-type labels inside `sub(s)`. Op-type label = the recorded `op` attribute on each node (or the node’s display name when `op` is absent) [P-4].\\n\\n   5.2 Secondary metrics (full graph G)\\n       M3  `|N|`                — total node count.\\n       M4  `|E|`                — total edge count.\\n       M5  `depth(G)`           — length of the longest directed path in the subgraph reachable from `loss`.\\n       M6  `cyc(G) = |E| − |N| + C(G)` where `C(G)` is the count of weakly connected components.\\n\\n   5.3 Optimizer-step subgraph extraction (mechanical recipe)\\n       Inputs: parsed graph `G`, parameter dict `params`.\\n       Steps (executor-implementable):\\n          (i)   Identify the set `P` of nodes whose label contains any of the kept names found by `list(dict(model.named_parameters()).keys())`. These are the parameter leaf nodes.\\n          (ii)  For each `p ∈ P`, locate the node reached by the edge out of `p` that represents `param + update` (heuristic: among immediate successors of `p`, select the one that contributes to the parameter’s incoming edge in the next forward; or, if a fog-of-war case, take the unique successor whose tensor-shape attribute matches `p`’s shape and is not `p` itself). If no such deterministic successor exists, mark the run as ambiguous and exclude it from M1/M2 [P-5].\\n          (iii) Identify the set of nodes `R` representing `param.grad` consumers — nodes whose op label is one of `AccumulateGrad`, or whose label string contains the parameter’s name and `.grad`. [Protocol placeholder [P-6] for the exact string match.]\\n          (iv) `sub(s) = induced_subgraph(G, R ∪ P ∪ ancestors(R) ∪ descendants(P_filtered))` restricted to nodes lying on a path from any node in `R` to any filtered successor of `P`.\\n       This is the primary slice — “param.grad consumption → next param.data write” — where the fused-vs-unfused claim is being tested.\\n\\n   5.4 Per-block partition (both for `sub(s)` and for the broader update region)\\n       Nodes within `sub(s)` are bucketed into five conceptual blocks based on op labels and graph locality:\\n         B1 moment-1 update       `(m ← β1·m + (1−β1)·g)`\\n         B2 moment-2 update       `(v ← β2·v + (1−β2)·g²)`\\n         B3 bias correction       `(m_hat, v_hat)`\\n         B4 parameter update      `(θ ← θ − lr·m_hat/(√v_hat+ε))`\\n         B5 weight-decay coupling `(if wd≠0: θ ← θ − lr·wd·θ)`\\n       Report `|N|·` and `op_diversity` *per block*, in addition to totals. Bucketing rules are heuristic op-label matches; ambiguous nodes are bucketed by cheapest-fit and flagged [P-7]. This per-block view is intended to localize where fusing helps (expected: B4 and the inter-block glue nodes collapse first).\\n\\n   5.5 Interpretability proxy\\n       Reported numerically as `op_diversity_sub` (M2) and qualitatively as the per-block partition table. A second interpretability sub-score `I = Σ_b (|N_b| · |op_types_b|)` provides a single scalar that penalizes both node count and op-type mix within the slice; report I but treat as exploratory.\\n\\n6. Controls (locked across all four conditions for every seed)\\n   - RNG: `torch.manual_seed(seed)` and `numpy.random.seed(seed)` set before model construction; `seed ∈ {0,1,2,3,4}` minimum — researcher's requirement of ≥5 paired seeds honored; we recommend 10 to stabilize the sign test [P-8].\\n   - Parameter snapshot: a `state_dict` clone is taken after initialization; the optimizer is constructed on a fresh clone for each condition so that the `step()` call mutates identical starting tensors.\\n   - Hyperparameters: identical `lr=1e-3`, `betas=(0.9, 0.999)`, `eps=1e-8`, `weight_decay=0.0` across all four conditions. `weight_decay=0.0` removes the wd coupling’s variability so B5 can be reported without ambiguity.\\n   - Forward/backward boundary: identical inputs, identical loss, exactly one `loss.backward()` and exactly one `optimizer.step()` before `make_dot`. `requires_grad=True` on all model parameters.\\n   - Device and dtype: CPU + float32 throughout. No autocast, no grad scaler.\\n   - `make_dot` invocation: identical kwarg signature; identical `params=` dict. Differences in the graph therefore come from the optimizer codepath, not the tracing setup.\\n   - Package installation: forbidden in this protocol (design-only, no network in bubblewrap).\\n\\n7. Analysis plan\\n   7.1 Aggregation. Per seed `s`, compute Δ_Ck(s) = metric(Ck) − metric(C0) for k ∈ {1, 2, ped}. Per-block and per-metric as above.\\n   7.2 Within-pair tests (per metric, per comparison Ck vs C0).\\n        - Matched-pairs sign test on Δ (non-parametric, matches the public guidance of “report matched-pairs sign test”).\\n        - Matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation as the effect size (matches the “report matched-pairs rank-biserial effect size” guidance).\\n        - Descriptive: per-seed values, median, IQR, min/max of Δ.\\n        - No claim is made of normality; no parametric test is used.\\n   7.3 Pre-registered expectation (recorded so later interpretation isn’t circular).\\n        - Expectation 1 (fused shrinks the slice): Δ_M1 < 0 for C1 vs C0 and C2 vs C0, with rank-biserial sign favoring “fused smaller”.\\n        - Expectation 2 (fused reduces op diversity): Δ_M2 < 0 for the same two comparisons.\\n        - Expectation 3 (pedagogical unfused is at least as large as PyTorch’s default): Δ_M1(C_ped − C0) ≥ 0 in magnitude sense (i.e., C_ped is the conceptual floor).\\n        Mark each as confirmed / disconfirmed against observed Δ at execution time; do not let the design be edited post-hoc to fit results.\\n   7.4 Qualitative layer. Produce a single comparison table with rows = the four conditions and columns = `|N_total|, |E_total|, depth, cyc, |N_sub|, op_diversity_sub, per-block |N| table, qualitative op-type list for B4`. This is the interpretability artifact a human reviewer reads.\\n   7.5 Within-TorchOpt comparison. Repeat 7.2 for C2 vs C1, treated as secondary, to assess whether the differentiable fused-meta path differs structurally from the standard fused path.\\n   7.6 Reporting standards. Report (i) every metric per condition per seed, (ii) Δ tables, (iii) effect sizes, (iv) qualitative table, (v) any extraction ambiguity flags from Section 5.3. No selective reporting.\\n\\n8. Threats to validity (recorded up front)\\n   - The optimizer-step subgraph extraction (Section 5.3) is heuristic; ambiguous runs are excluded and reported.\\n   - C_ped depends on the executor’s from-scratch implementation; specify that it must mirror the canonical Adam math byte-for-byte (no fused trig, no in-place cleverness).\\n   - TorchOpt’s fused implementation may invoke kernels that are no-ops under `make_dot` tracing (graph capture ≠ kernel fusion). The design treats `make_dot`’s graph as the unit of comparison regardless — that is exactly the question’s frame.\\n   - No GPU rules out the most aggressive fused-Adam backends; results are CPU-only and may not generalize. Recorded as scope of inference.\\n   - The bubblewrap environment is design-only; producing the metric values is out of scope here and is flagged in the feasibility note.\\n\\n9. Feasibility note (bubblewrap environment)\\n   - Confirmed via inspection: `/workspace` contains only `task.json` (466 B). No `track.md` file exists on disk; the only reference is the string `Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.` inside `task.json.documents`.\\n   - Python 3.12.3 and pip 24.0 are present; `import torch`, `import torchviz`, `import torchopt` all fail with `ModuleNotFoundError`. No source trees for any of these libraries are present on the filesystem (searched to depth 7).\\n   - Network is disabled; no package installation is attempted. The protocol forbids installs regardless.\\n   - Therefore this deliverable is a written experimental design with mechanically operationalized metrics, not an executed experiment. Anything that requires executing PyTorch / TorchOpt / TorchViz is described as an executor-visible procedure and labeled with protocol placeholders [P-1]–[P-8] below.\\n\\n10. Assumptions (per researcher's instruction, restated verbatim)\\n   (a) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Scope both TorchOpt Adam entry points individually in the design: `torchopt.Adam` as the standard fused optimizer and `torchopt.MetaAdam` (or whichever current alias wraps the differentiable / fused-meta variant TorchOpt ships) as a second condition. The reason is that 'fusing operations within Adam' can mean either fused-state fused-step kernels (plain Adam) or fused-meta-differentiable stepping (MetaAdam); both are fair readings and the design should not silently pick one.\\\"\\n   (b) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Fix the TorchViz call to `torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters()))` in both conditions so the rendered graph covers the autograd edges into every leaf parameter; this is the slice where the optimizer-step subgraph actually attaches, and it matches the question's 'gradient graph' wording.\\\"\\n   (c) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Interpret 'gradient graph' as the full autograd graph visible to `make_dot` under (b), i.e. forward + backward edges terminating at parameter leaves. Treat the optimizer-step subgraph (param.grad consumption → next param.data write) as the primary slicing region for the fused-vs-unfused claim, and forward/backward portions as secondary. Promote optimizer-subgraph node count and operator diversity to primary metrics; full-graph node/edge count, depth, and cyclomatic complexity become secondary.\\\"\\n   (d) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Mirror TorchOpt's actual codepath as the primary C1 implementation, and additionally include a third condition C_ped (C-pedagogical): an explicit unfused re-implementation of the Adam update in plain PyTorch ops, used as a sanity reference so the comparison is not 'whatever PyTorch's Adam emits' vs 'TorchOpt'. This makes the interpretability claim testable without conflating framework defaults with the conceptual unfused baseline.\\\"\\n   These are explicitly flagged as researcher assumptions, not source-confirmed facts, so a reader can audit them.\\n\\n11. Other design assumptions (non-verbatim, recorded for audit)\\n   - D.1 The four conditions are sufficient; no other Adam-fused variant (e.g., Apex, functorch's `torch.compile` Adam) is in scope.\\n   - D.2 `weight_decay=0.0` is locked to remove a confound from B5 reporting; this is a design choice, not a default of any library.\\n   - D.3 Op-type labels produced by `make_dot` are sufficiently stable across conditions to support the per-block bucketing in Section 5.4; if not, bucketing rules in [P-7] apply.\\n   - D.4 ≥5 paired seeds; ≥10 recommended. The sign test and rank-biserial are valid at this sample size but coarse.\\n   - D.5 CPU-only inference scope. Results may not transfer to GPU-fused backends.\\n   - D.6 No claim is made of running code in this deliverable; the protocol above is a specification an executor can implement mechanically.\\n\\n12. Protocol placeholders to be confirmed at execution time\\n   [P-1] Exact current TorchOpt alias for MetaAdam (e.g., `torchopt.MetaAdam`, `torchopt.optim.meta.MetaAdam`).\\n   [P-2] Exact supported kwarg set of `torchviz.make_dot` for the executor’s installed version; the call is `torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters()))`.\\n   [P-3] Parser choice for extracting node/edge counts (e.g., `pydot` from `dot.source()`).\\n   [P-4] Definition of `op-type label` if `make_dot` strips op attributes under chosen kwargs; default rule documented in 5.1.\\n   [P-5] Eligibility rule when an immediate-successor heuristic fails; default is to exclude the run.\\n   [P-6] String-match rules for `AccumulateGrad` / param.grad consumer identification in the rendered graph.\\n   [P-7] Per-block bucketing defaults; ambiguous nodes are cheapest-fit and flagged.\\n   [P-8] Final seed count; minimum is 5, recommended is 10.\\n\\nEnd of design. Awaiting reviewer feedback on metric definitions, controls, or the placeholders before any execution attempt.\",\"evidence\":\"(1) Workspace inspection: `ls -laR /workspace` returns a single 0600 file `task.json` (466 B); no other files exist, including no `track.md`. The only reference to `track.md` is the string `Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.` inside `task.json.documents`. (2) Local availability of TorchOpt / TorchViz / torch: `find / -maxdepth 7 -iname torchopt/...` returned zero directory matches; Python 3.12.3 + pip 24.0 are present but `import torch`, `import torchviz`, `import torchopt` all fail with `ModuleNotFoundError`; no install attempted due to the no-network rule. (3) Researcher authorizations captured verbatim in Section 10(a)–(d) per the explicit instruction to restate them so a reviewer can audit. (4) No TorchOpt or TorchViz internals are fabricated beyond the API surface needed to specify the entry points (Adam, MetaAdam, `make_dot(loss, params=...)`); any detail that cannot be stated confidently is marked with protocol placeholders [P-1]–[P-8]. (5) No experiment is claimed to have run; the design specifies procedures at a level a future executor could implement mechanically.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"D.6 This deliverable is design-only; no execution is claimed. Protocol placeholders [P-1]–[P-8] are flagged for executor-time confirmation.</item>\",\"$text\":\"D.5 CPU-only scope; results are not generalized to GPU-fused backends.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.4 ≥5 paired seeds (≥10 recommended); statistical claims are limited to sign test + matched-pairs rank-biserial.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.3 `make_dot` op labels are sufficiently stable across conditions for the per-block bucketing rules to apply; ambiguity is handled by [P-7].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.2 `weight_decay=0.0` is locked across all conditions to control B5 reporting.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.1 Four conditions (C0, C1=Ctorchopt.Adam, C2=Ctorchopt.MetaAdam, C_ped) are the complete scope; no other fused-Adam variant is in scope.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Researcher assumptions (a)–(d) as recorded verbatim in Section 10.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Final seed count choice within the ≥5–10 recommended band [P-8].</item>\",\"$text\":\"Per-block bucketing default-overlap behavior [P-7].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"String-match rules for identifying `param.grad` consumers in the rendered graph [P-6].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Heuristic for identifying the `param + update` successor when multiple immediate successors of a parameter leaf exist [P-5].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether op-type labels survive `make_dot` under the locked kwarg shape [P-4].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Parser used to extract node/edge counts from `dot` [P-3].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Exact supported kwarg set of `torchviz.make_dot` in the executor’s installed version [P-2] and whether `params=` triggers the leaf-parameter edge rendering.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Current TorchOpt alias for the differentiable fused-meta Adam entry point [P-1].</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-adaea59d-a741-4983-8795-f839faa09ae6", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
