-TorchOpt comparison. Repeat 7.2 for C2 vs C1, treated as secondary, to assess whether the differentiable fused-meta path differs structurally from the standard fused path.\\n   7.6 Reporting standards. Report (i) every metric per condition per seed, (ii) d tables, (iii) effect sizes, (iv) qualitative table, (v) any extraction ambiguity flags from Section 5.3. No selective reporting.\\n\\n8. Threats to validity (recorded up front)\\n   - The optimizer-step subgraph extraction (Section 5.3) is heuristic; ambiguous runs are excluded and reported.\\n   - C_ped depends on the executor's from-scratch implementation; specify that it must mirror the canonical Adam math byte-for-byte (no fused trig, no in-place cleverness).\\n   - TorchOpt's fused implementation may invoke kernels that are no-ops under make_dot tracing (graph capture is not the same as kernel fusion). The design treats make_dot's graph as the unit of comparison regardless — that is exactly the question's frame.\\n   - No GPU rules out the most aggressive fused-Adam backends; results are CPU-only and may not generalize. Recorded as scope of inference.\\n   - The bubblewrap environment is design-only; producing the metric values is out of scope here and is flagged in the feasibility note.\\n\\n9. Feasibility note (bubblewrap environment)\\n   - Confirmed via inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (466 B). No track.md file exists on disk; the only reference is the string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" inside task.json.documents.\\n   - Python 3.12.3 and pip 24.0 are present; import torch, import torchviz, import torchopt all fail with ModuleNotFoundError. No source trees for any of these libraries are present on the filesystem (searched to depth 7).\\n   - Network is disabled; no package installation is attempted. The protocol forbids installs regardless.\\n   - Therefore this deliverable is a written experimental design with mechanically operationalized metrics, not an executed experiment. Anything that requires executing PyTorch / TorchOpt / TorchViz is described as an executor-visible procedure and labeled with protocol placeholders [P-1] through [P-8] below.\\n\\n10. Assumptions (per researcher's instruction, restated verbatim)\\n   (a) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Scope both TorchOpt Adam entry points individually in the design: torchopt.Adam as the standard fused optimizer and torchopt.MetaAdam (or whichever current alias wraps the differentiable / fused-meta variant TorchOpt ships) as a second condition. The reason is that 'fusing operations within Adam' can mean either fused-state fused-step kernels (plain Adam) or fused-meta-differentiable stepping (MetaAdam); both are fair readings and the design should not silently pick one.\\\"\\n   (b) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Fix the TorchViz call to torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters())) in both conditions so the rendered graph covers the autograd edges into every leaf parameter; this is the slice where the optimizer-step subgraph actually attaches, and it matches the question's 'gradient graph' wording.\\\"\\n   (c) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Interpret 'gradient graph' as the full autograd graph visible to make_dot under (b), i.e. forward + backward edges terminating at parameter leaves. Treat the optimizer-step subgraph (param.grad consumption to next param.data write) as the primary slicing region for the fused-vs-unfused claim, and forward/backward portions as secondary. Promote optimizer-subgraph node count and operator diversity to primary metrics; full-graph node/edge count, depth, and cyclomatic complexity become secondary.\\\"\\n   (d) Researcher assumption (restated verbatim): \\\"Mirror TorchOpt's actual codepath as the primary C1 implementation, and additionally include a third condition C_ped (C-pedagogical): an explicit unfused re-implementation of the Adam update in plain PyTorch ops, used as a sanity reference so the comparison is not 'whatever PyTorch's Adam emits' vs 'TorchOpt'. This makes the interpretability claim testable without conflating framework defaults with the conceptual unfused baseline.\\\"\\n   These are explicitly flagged as researcher assumptions, not source-confirmed facts, so a reader can audit them.\\n\\n11. Other design assumptions (non-verbatim, recorded for audit, D.1 through D.6)\\n   - D.1 Four conditions (C0, C1=torchopt.Adam, C2=torchopt.MetaAdam, C_ped) are sufficient; no other Adam-fused variant (e.g., Apex, functorch's torch.compile Adam) is in scope.\\n   - D.2 weight_decay=0.0 is locked to remove a confound from B5 reporting; this is a design choice, not a default of any library.\\n   - D.3 Op-type labels produced by make_dot are sufficiently stable across conditions to support the per-block bucketing in Section 5.4; if not, bucketing rules in [P-7] apply.\\n   - D.4 At least 5 paired seeds; 10 recommended. The sign test and rank-biserial are valid at this sample size but coarse.\\n   - D.5 CPU-only inference scope. Results may not transfer to GPU-fused backends.\\n   - D.6 No claim is made of running code in this deliverable; the protocol above is a specification an executor can implement mechanically.\\n\\n12. Protocol placeholders to be confirmed at execution time\\n   [P-1] Exact current TorchOpt alias for MetaAdam (e.g., torchopt.MetaAdam, torchopt.optim.meta.MetaAdam).\\n   [P-2] Exact supported kwarg set of torchviz.make_dot for the executor's installed version; the canonical call is torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters())).\\n   [P-3] Parser choice for extracting node/edge counts (e.g., pydot from dot.source()).\\n   [P-4] Definition of \\\"op-type label\\\" if make_dot strips op attributes under chosen kwargs; default rule documented in 5.1.\\n   [P-5] Eligibility rule when an immediate-successor heuristic fails; default is to exclude the run.\\n   [P-6] String-match rules for AccumulateGrad and param.grad consumer identification in the rendered graph.\\n   [P-7] Per-block bucketing defaults; ambiguous nodes are cheapest-fit and flagged.\\n   [P-8] Final seed count; minimum is 5, recommended is 10.\\n\\nEnd of design. Awaiting reviewer feedback on metric definitions, controls, or the placeholders before any execution attempt.\",\"evidence\":\"(1) Workspace inspection: ls -laR /workspace returns a single 0600 file task.json (466 B); no other files exist, including no track.md. The only reference to track.md is the string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" inside task.json.documents. (2) Local availability of TorchOpt / TorchViz / torch: find up to depth 7 returned zero directory matches; Python 3.12.3 and pip 24.0 are present but import torch, import torchviz, import torchopt all fail with ModuleNotFoundError; no install attempted (no-network rule). (3) Researcher authorizations captured verbatim in Section 10(a)-(d) per explicit instruction. (4) No TorchOpt / TorchViz internals fabricated beyond the entry points needed to specify the API surface; any detail that cannot be stated confidently is marked with protocol placeholders [P-1]-[P-8]. (5) No execution is claimed; the design specifies procedures at a level a future executor could implement mechanically.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"D.6 This deliverable is design-only; no execution is claimed. Protocol placeholders [P-1] through [P-8] are flagged for executor-time confirmation.</item>\",\"$text\":\"D.5 CPU-only scope; results are not generalized to GPU-fused backends.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.4 At least 5 paired seeds (10 recommended); statistical claims are limited to the sign test and matched-pairs rank-biserial.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.3 make_dot op labels are sufficiently stable across conditions for the per-block bucketing rules to apply; ambiguity is handled by [P-7].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.2 weight_decay=0.0 is locked across all conditions to control B5 reporting.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.1 Four conditions (C0, C1=torchopt.Adam, C2=torchopt.MetaAdam, C_ped) are the complete scope; no other fused-Adam variant is in scope.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Researcher assumptions (a) through (d) recorded verbatim in Section 10 are taken as explicit assumptions of this design.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Final seed count choice within the 5-10 recommended band [P-8].</item>\",\"$text\":\"Per-block bucketing default-overlap behavior [P-7].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"String-match rules for identifying param.grad consumers in the rendered graph [P-6].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Heuristic for identifying the param + update successor when multiple immediate successors of a parameter leaf exist [P-5].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Whether op-type labels survive make_dot under the locked kwarg shape [P-4].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Parser used to extract node/edge counts from dot [P-3].</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Exact supported kwarg set of torchviz.make_dot in the executor's installed version [P-2] and whether params= triggers the leaf-parameter edge rendering.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"Current TorchOpt alias for the differentiable fused-meta Adam entry point [P-1].</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-ec199202-91c7-441e-b8e4-7eed11e59cc4", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 29}
