{"id": "cbc90350-0749-45c8-b407-b6b4b5f1e415", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:00:43.493262", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "0231a32a-2641-4a52-8fbb-d3a76af76305", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-9ed1095f-e90e-4714-961c-edcd313332f1", "tool_call": {"id": "call-9ed1095f-e90e-4714-961c-edcd313332f1", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Final design delivered: 4 conditions, primary metrics, paired analysis\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"Final experiment design — design-only deliverable. No code executed; no experiment claimed to have run.\\n\\n================================================================\\nTitle: Fused Adam (TorchOpt) vs Unfused Adam — Gradient/Compute Graph Complexity and Interpretability\\n================================================================\\n\\n1. Research question (as supplied, restated). Does fusing operations within the Adam optimizer (as done in TorchOpt) result in a less complex and more interpretable gradient graph compared to the output from TorchViz?\\n\\n2. Conditions (four, paired on seed and parameter snapshot).\\n   C0: torch.optim.Adam baseline — one step(); PyTorch's default unfused reference.\\n   C1: torchopt.Adam — one step(); standard fused optimizer; primary TorchOpt mirror.\\n   C2: torchopt.MetaAdam (or current equivalent alias for the fused-meta / differentiable variant) — one step(); exactly which alias is a protocol-level placeholder [P-1].\\n   C_ped: pedagogical unfused re-implementation of the Adam update in plain PyTorch elementwise ops; canonical (m, v, m_hat, v_hat, theta) in order; conceptual unfused floor independent of framework defaults.\\n\\n   Rationale for the four-way design. The question conflates two readings of \\\"fusing operations within Adam\\\" (state-fused step kernels for plain Adam, and fused-meta-differentiable stepping); including both is the only way to avoid silently preferring one. C_ped exists so the interpretability comparison is conceptually unfused vs fused, not silently torch.optim.Adam defaults vs TorchOpt.\\n\\n3. Reference model and inputs (locked).\\n   - Model: 3-layer MLP, hidden size 64, activation ReLU, final linear head; ~20k parameters so the rendered DOT graph is human-inspectable but non-trivial. Exact layer widths can be re-pinned at execution time without changing metric definitions.\\n   - Inputs: fixed synthetic regression batch x of shape (B=32, D=16), y = W* x + eps; seed pinned.\\n   - Loss: MSE between model output and y. One forward, one loss.backward(), one optimizer step; no extra training iteration.\\n   - Device: CPU only. Dtype: float32. No CUDA, no AMP.\\n\\n4. Graph acquisition (identical call shape across all four conditions).\\n   dot = torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters())) — kwarg shape [P-2]. Same call byte-for-byte in C0, C1, C2, C_ped; differs only in the optimizer used for the step. The rendered graph is the full autograd graph visible to make_dot under this call: forward edges + backward edges terminating at parameter leaves. Per researcher resolutions (b) and (c), the \\\"gradient graph\\\" is this full graph; the optimizer-step subgraph is the primary slice within it.\\n\\n5. Metrics (operationalized mechanically).\\n\\n   Notation. G = (N, E) is the rendered graph; sub(s) is the optimizer-step subgraph of Section 5.3. All counts are computed from dot.source() parsed with pydot [P-3]. Each metric is computed per-seed per-condition; later aggregated.\\n\\n   5.1 Primary metrics (optimizer-step subgraph only).\\n       M1  |N_sub|          — number of nodes inside sub(s).\\n       M2  op_diversity_sub — number of distinct op-type labels inside sub(s). Op-type label is the recorded \\\"op\\\" attribute on each node, or the node's display name when \\\"op\\\" is absent [P-4].\\n\\n   5.2 Secondary metrics (full graph G).\\n       M3  |N|        — total node count.\\n       M4  |E|        — total edge count.\\n       M5  depth(G)   — length of the longest directed path in the subgraph reachable from loss.\\n       M6  cyc(G)     — |E| - |N| + C(G) where C(G) is the count of weakly connected components.\\n\\n   5.3 Optimizer-step subgraph extraction (mechanical recipe).\\n       Inputs: parsed graph G; parameter dict params.\\n         (i)   Identify P = nodes whose label contains any name in dict(model.named_parameters()).keys() (parameter leaves).\\n         (ii)  For each p in P, locate the node reached by \\\"param + update\\\": among immediate successors of p, take the one whose shape attribute matches p and is not p itself. If no such deterministic successor exists, mark the run ambiguous and exclude it from M1/M2 [P-5].\\n         (iii) Identify R = param.grad consumers: nodes whose op label is AccumulateGrad, or whose label string contains the parameter's name and \\\".grad\\\" [P-6].\\n         (iv) sub(s) = induced_subgraph(G, R ∪ P ∪ ancestors(R) ∪ filtered_descendants(P)), restricted to nodes lying on a path from any node in R to any filtered successor of P.\\n       This is the primary slice — param.grad consumption to next param.data write — where the fused-vs-unfused claim is being tested.\\n\\n   5.4 Per-block partition (of sub(s)).\\n       B1 moment-1 update       (m    = beta1*m + (1-beta1)*g)\\n       B2 moment-2 update       (v    = beta2*v + (1-beta2)*g^2)\\n       B3 bias correction       (m_hat = m/(1-beta1^t); v_hat = v/(1-beta2^t))\\n       B4 parameter update      (theta = theta - lr*m_hat/(sqrt(v_hat)+eps))\\n       B5 weight-decay coupling (theta = theta - lr*wd*theta; with wd=0 this block is empty by construction)\\n       Bucketing is heuristic by op-label; ambiguous nodes cheapest-fit and flagged [P-7]. Report |N| and op_diversity per block. Per-block view localizes where fusing helps (expected: B4 and inter-block glue nodes collapse first).\\n\\n   5.5 Interpretability proxies.\\n       Primary proxy: op_diversity_sub (M2). Secondary scalar: I = sum_b (|N_b| * |op_types_b|). Report both; treat I as exploratory.\\n\\n6. Controls (locked across all conditions for every seed).\\n   - RNG: torch.manual_seed(seed) and numpy.random.seed(seed) before model construction; seed in {0..4} minimum (5 seeds, per researcher's at-least-5 rule); 10 recommended [P-8].\\n   - Parameter snapshot: state_dict clone after init; optimizer built on a fresh clone per condition so step() mutates identical starting tensors.\\n   - Hyperparameters: identical lr=1e-3, betas=(0.9, 0.999), eps=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0 across all four conditions. wd=0 locks B5 to empty and removes a confound.\\n   - Boundary: identical inputs, identical loss, exactly one loss.backward() and exactly one optimizer.step() before make_dot. requires_grad=True on all model parameters.\\n   - Device and dtype: CPU + float32 throughout. No autocast, no grad scaler.\\n   - make_dot: identical kwarg signature; identical params= dict. Differences in the graph therefore come from the optimizer codepath, not the tracing setup.\\n   - Package installation: forbidden in this protocol (design-only, no network in bubblewrap).\\n\\n7. Analysis plan.\\n   7.1 Aggregation. Per seed s, d_Ck(s) = metric(Ck) - metric(C0) for k in {1, 2, ped}. For both per-block and whole-graph metrics.\\n   7.2 Within-pair tests (per metric, per comparison Ck vs C0).\\n        - Matched-pairs sign test on d (matches the researcher's \\\"report matched-pairs sign test\\\" guidance).\\n        - Matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation as effect size (matches the \\\"report matched-pairs rank-biserial effect size\\\" guidance).\\n        - Descriptive: per-seed values, median, IQR, min/max of d.\\n        - No normality assumed; no parametric test used.\\n   7.3 Pre-registered expectations (recorded so later interpretation is not circular).\\n        - E1 (fused shrinks the slice): d_M1 < 0 for C1 vs C0 and C2 vs C0.\\n        - E2 (fused reduces op diversity): d_M2 < 0 for the same comparisons.\\n        - E3 (C_ped is the conceptual floor): |d_M1(C_ped - C0)| >= |d_M1(C1 - C0)| in magnitude.\\n        Each marked confirmed or disconfirmed at execution time; no post-hoc editing of the design.\\n   7.4 Qualitative layer. Single comparison table: rows = four conditions; columns = |N_total|, |E_total|, depth, cyc, |N_sub|, op_diversity_sub, per-block |N|, qualitative op-type list for B4. This is the interpretability artifact a human reviewer reads.\\n   7.5 Within-TorchOpt comparison. Repeat 7.2 for C2 vs C1 (secondary) to assess whether the differentiable fused-meta path differs structurally from the standard fused path.\\n   7.6 Reporting standards. Report (i) every metric per condition per seed, (ii) d tables, (iii) effect sizes, (iv) qualitative table, (v) any extraction ambiguity flags from 5.3. No selective reporting.\\n\\n8. Threats to validity (recorded up front).\\n   - Optimizer-step subgraph extraction (5.3) is heuristic; ambiguous runs excluded and reported.\\n   - C_ped depends on executor's from-scratch implementation; specify canonical Adam math, no fused trig, no in-place cleverness.\\n   - Graph capture is not the same as kernel fusion; TorchOpt's fused implementation may map onto make_dot in either collapsed or expanded form. The design treats make_dot's graph as the unit of comparison regardless — that is the question's frame.\\n   - No GPU; CPU-only; results may not generalize to GPU-fused backends.\\n   - Bubblewrap is design-only; producing metric values is out of scope here, flagged in the feasibility note.\\n\\n9. Feasibility note (bubblewrap).\\n   - Confirmed by inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (466 B). No track.md file exists on disk; the only reference is the string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" inside task.json.documents.\\n   - Python 3.12.3 and pip 24.0 are present; import torch, import torchviz, import torchopt all fail with ModuleNotFoundError. No