{"id": "9d6ce204-b7d1-411a-ada1-4aa87a81f69c", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:02:42.580387", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "2458978b-f9ea-4d43-ac3e-b7e451070e90", "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-8243c5b7-a3ae-41f6-8190-74ade58690b2", "error": "Error validating tool 'end_researcher_turn': 2 validation errors for ReviewHandoffAction\nassumptions.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...confirmation.</item>\\n'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type\nuncertainties.0\n  Input should be a valid string [type=string_type, input_value={'item': {'item': {'item'...d band [P-8].</item>\\n'}, input_type=dict]\n    For further information visit https://errors.pydantic.dev/2.13/v/string_type. Parameters provided: ['stage', 'answer', 'evidence', 'assumptions', 'uncertainties']", "classification": {"kind": "agent_action", "retryable": true, "user_action": "retry", "error_id": null}, "kind": "AgentErrorEvent", "source_event_index": 37}
{"id": "b883957c-f3f9-486d-b722-71453a28f9e6", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T18:04:50.757415", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "9d6ce204-b7d1-411a-ada1-4aa87a81f69c", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-2b27dfb3-8ac0-4ae0-ad7a-330a297cb626", "tool_call": {"id": "call-2b27dfb3-8ac0-4ae0-ad7a-330a297cb626", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Final design with arrays on single lines\",\"stage\":\"result\",\"answer\":\"Final experiment design — design-only deliverable. No code executed; no experiment claimed to have run.\\n\\n================================================================\\nTitle: Fused Adam (TorchOpt) vs Unfused Adam — Gradient/Compute Graph Complexity and Interpretability\\n================================================================\\n\\n1. Research question (restated). Does fusing operations within the Adam optimizer (as done in TorchOpt) result in a less complex and more interpretable gradient graph compared to the output from TorchViz?\\n\\n2. Conditions (four, paired on seed and parameter snapshot).\\n   C0: torch.optim.Adam baseline, one step(), PyTorch default unfused reference.\\n   C1: torchopt.Adam, one step(), standard fused optimizer, primary TorchOpt mirror.\\n   C2: torchopt.MetaAdam (or current alias for the differentiable fused-meta variant), one step(); exact alias is a protocol placeholder P-1.\\n   C_ped: pedagogical unfused re-implementation of the Adam update in plain PyTorch elementwise ops; canonical (m, v, m_hat, v_hat, theta) in order; conceptual unfused floor independent of framework defaults.\\n   Rationale. The question conflates two readings of fusing operations within Adam (state-fused step kernels vs fused-meta-differentiable stepping); including both avoids silent preference. C_ped ensures the interpretability comparison is conceptually unfused vs fused rather than torch.optim.Adam defaults vs TorchOpt.\\n\\n3. Reference model and inputs (locked).\\n   - Model: 3-layer MLP, hidden size 64, activation ReLU, final linear head, about 20k parameters so the rendered DOT graph is human-inspectable but non-trivial. Exact widths can be re-pinned at execution time without changing metric definitions.\\n   - Inputs: fixed synthetic regression batch x of shape (B=32, D=16), y = W* x + eps, seed pinned.\\n   - Loss: MSE between model output and y. One forward, one loss.backward(), one optimizer step; no extra training iteration.\\n   - Device: CPU only. Dtype: float32. No CUDA, no AMP.\\n\\n4. Graph acquisition. dot = torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters())) (kwarg shape P-2). Same call byte-for-byte across C0, C1, C2, C_ped; differs only in the optimizer used for the step. Rendered graph is the full autograd graph visible to make_dot under this call: forward edges plus backward edges terminating at parameter leaves. Per researcher resolutions (b) and (c), the gradient graph is this full graph; the optimizer-step subgraph is the primary slice within it.\\n\\n5. Metrics (operationalized mechanically).\\n   Notation. G = (N, E) is the rendered graph; sub(s) is the optimizer-step subgraph of Section 5.3. Counts computed from dot.source() parsed with pydot (P-3). Each metric computed per-seed per-condition, then aggregated.\\n   5.1 Primary metrics (optimizer-step subgraph only).\\n       M1 = |N_sub|: number of nodes inside sub(s).\\n       M2 = op_diversity_sub: number of distinct op-type labels inside sub(s). Op-type label is the recorded op attribute on each node, or the node display name when op is absent (P-4).\\n   5.2 Secondary metrics (full graph G).\\n       M3 = |N|: total node count.\\n       M4 = |E|: total edge count.\\n       M5 = depth(G): length of the longest directed path in the subgraph reachable from loss.\\n       M6 = cyc(G): |E| - |N| + C(G) where C(G) is the count of weakly connected components.\\n   5.3 Optimizer-step subgraph extraction (mechanical recipe).\\n       Inputs: parsed graph G; parameter dict params.\\n         (i)   Identify the set P of nodes whose label contains any name from dict(model.named_parameters()).keys() (the parameter leaves).\\n         (ii)  For each p in P, locate the node reached by param + update: among immediate successors of p, take the one whose shape attribute matches p and is not p itself. If no such deterministic successor exists, mark the run ambiguous and exclude it from M1/M2 (P-5).\\n         (iii) Identify the set R of param.grad consumers: nodes whose op label is AccumulateGrad, or whose label string contains the parameter name and .grad (P-6).\\n         (iv) sub(s) = induced_subgraph(G, R union P union ancestors(R) union filtered_descendants(P)), restricted to nodes lying on a path from any node in R to any filtered successor of P.\\n       This is the primary slice: param.grad consumption to next param.data write, where the fused-vs-unfused claim is being tested.\\n   5.4 Per-block partition of sub(s).\\n       B1 moment-1 update: m = beta1*m + (1-beta1)*g\\n       B2 moment-2 update: v = beta2*v + (1-beta2)*g^2\\n       B3 bias correction: m_hat = m/(1-beta1^t); v_hat = v/(1-beta2^t)\\n       B4 parameter update: theta = theta - lr*m_hat/(sqrt(v_hat)+eps)\\n       B5 weight-decay coupling: theta = theta - lr*wd*theta; with wd=0 this block is empty by construction.\\n       Bucketing is heuristic by op-label; ambiguous nodes cheapest-fit and flagged (P-7). Report |N| and op_diversity per block; the per-block view localizes where fusing helps (expected: B4 and inter-block glue nodes collapse first).\\n   5.5 Interpretability proxies.\\n       Primary proxy is op_diversity_sub (M2). Secondary scalar I = sum_b (|N_b| * |op_types_b|); report both; treat I as exploratory.\\n\\n6. Controls (locked across all conditions for every seed).\\n   - RNG: torch.manual_seed(seed) and numpy.random.seed(seed) before model construction; seed in {0..4} minimum (resolves the at-least-5 rule); 10 recommended (P-8).\\n   - Parameter snapshot: state_dict clone after init; optimizer built on a fresh clone per condition so step() mutates identical starting tensors.\\n   - Hyperparameters: identical lr=1e-3, betas=(0.9, 0.999), eps=1e-8, weight_decay=0.0 across all four conditions. wd=0 locks B5 to empty and removes a confound.\\n   - Boundary: identical inputs, identical loss, exactly one loss.backward() and exactly one optimizer.step() before make_dot. requires_grad=True on all model parameters.\\n   - Device and dtype: CPU and float32 throughout. No autocast, no grad scaler.\\n   - make_dot call: identical kwarg signature and identical params dict. Any difference in the graph therefore comes from the optimizer codepath, not the tracing setup.\\n   - Package installation: forbidden in this protocol (design-only, no network in bubblewrap).\\n\\n7. Analysis plan.\\n   7.1 Aggregation. Per seed s, d_Ck(s) = metric(Ck) - metric(C0) for k in {1, 2, ped}, per-block and whole-graph.\\n   7.2 Within-pair tests per metric, per comparison Ck vs C0.\\n       - Matched-pairs sign test on d (matches the report matched-pairs sign test guidance).\\n       - Matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation as effect size (matches the report matched-pairs rank-biserial effect size guidance).\\n       - Descriptive: per-seed values, median, IQR, min and max of d.\\n       - No normality assumption; no parametric test.\\n   7.3 Pre-registered expectations (recorded so later interpretation is not circular).\\n       E1 (fused shrinks the slice): d_M1 < 0 for C1 vs C0 and C2 vs C0.\\n       E2 (fused reduces op diversity): d_M2 < 0 for the same comparisons.\\n       E3 (C_ped is the conceptual floor): |d_M1(C_ped - C0)| >= |d_M1(C1 - C0)| in magnitude sense.\\n       Each marked confirmed or disconfirmed at execution time; no post-hoc editing of the design.\\n   7.4 Qualitative operator-level walkthrough table. Rows are the four conditions (C0, C1, C2, C_ped). Columns are |N_total|, |E_total|, depth(G), cyc(G), |N_sub|, op_diversity_sub, per-block |N| table (B1..B5), and a qualitative op-type list for B4 (the parameter-update block where fusion is expected to bite hardest). This is the interpretability artifact a human reviewer reads.\\n   7.5 Within-TorchOpt comparison. Repeat 7.2 for C2 vs C1, secondary, to assess whether the differentiable fused-meta path differs structurally from the standard fused path.\\n   7.6 Reporting standards. Report (i) every metric per condition per seed, (ii) d tables, (iii) effect sizes, (iv) qualitative walkthrough table, (v) any extraction ambiguity flags from Section 5.3. No selective reporting.\\n\\n8. Threats to validity.\\n   - Optimizer-step subgraph extraction (5.3) is heuristic; ambiguous runs are excluded and reported.\\n   - C_ped depends on the executor's from-scratch implementation; specify canonical Adam math, no fused trig, no in-place cleverness.\\n   - Graph capture is not the same as kernel fusion; TorchOpt's fused implementation may map onto make_dot in either collapsed or expanded form. The design treats make_dot's graph as the unit of comparison regardless — that is the question's frame.\\n   - No GPU; CPU-only; results may not generalize to GPU-fused backends.\\n   - Bubblewrap is design-only; producing metric values is out of scope here, flagged in the feasibility note.\\n\\n9. Feasibility note (bubblewrap).\\n   - Confirmed by inspection: /workspace contains only task.json (466 B). No track.md file exists on disk; the only reference is the string Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required. inside task.json.documents.\\n   - Python 3.12.3 and pip 24.0 are present; import torch, import torchviz, import torchopt all fail with ModuleNotFoundError. No source trees for any of these libraries exist on the filesystem (searched to depth 7).\\n   - Network is disabled; no package installation is attempted, and the protocol forbids installs regardless.\\n   - No GPU; CPU-only (cpu_affinity_count=24 in environment, but only CPU inference is in scope).\\n   - Therefore this deliverable is a written experimental design with mechanically operationalized metrics, not an executed experiment. Anything that requires running PyTorch, TorchOpt, or TorchViz is described as an executor-visible procedure and labeled with protocol placeholders P-1 through P-8 to be confirmed at execution time. No TorchOpt or TorchViz implementation detail is fabricated beyond the entry point required by the design; any further detail is a placeholder.\\n\\n10. Researcher assumptions (restated verbatim, for audit, per instruction).\\n   (a) \\\"Scope both TorchOpt Adam entry points individually in the design: torchopt.Adam as the standard fused optimizer and torchopt.MetaAdam (or whichever current alias wraps the differentiable / fused-meta variant TorchOpt ships) as a second condition. The reason is that 'fusing operations within Adam' can mean either fused-state fused-step kernels (plain Adam) or fused-meta-differentiable stepping (MetaAdam); both are fair readings and the design should not silently pick one.\\\"\\n   (b) \\\"Fix the TorchViz call to torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters())) in both conditions so the rendered graph covers the autograd edges into every leaf parameter; this is the slice where the optimizer-step subgraph actually attaches, and it matches the question's 'gradient graph' wording.\\\"\\n   (c) \\\"Interpret 'gradient graph' as the full autograd graph visible to make_dot under (b), i.e. forward + backward edges terminating at parameter leaves. Treat the optimizer-step subgraph (param.grad consumption to next param.data write) as the primary slicing region for the fused-vs-unfused claim, and forward/backward portions as secondary. Promote optimizer-subgraph node count and operator diversity to primary metrics; full-graph node/edge count, depth, and cyclomatic complexity become secondary.\\\"\\n   (d) \\\"Mirror TorchOpt's actual codepath as the primary C1 implementation, and additionally include a third condition C_ped (C-pedagogical): an explicit unfused re-implementation of the Adam update in plain PyTorch ops, used as a sanity reference so the comparison is not 'whatever PyTorch's Adam emits' vs 'TorchOpt'. This makes the interpretability claim testable without conflating framework defaults with the conceptual unfused baseline.\\\"\\n   These are researcher-provided assumptions, explicitly flagged as not source-confirmed and included verbatim for audit.\\n\\n11. Other design assumptions (non-verbatim, D.1..D.6).\\n   D.1 The four conditions are the complete scope; no other Adam-fused variant (e.g., Apex, functorch torch.compile Adam) is in scope.\\n   D.2 weight_decay=0.0 is locked to remove a B5 confound; this is a design choice rather than a library default.\\n   D.3 Op labels from make_dot are stable enough across conditions for the per-block bucketing in 5.4; if not, P-7 default applies.\\n   D.4 At least 5 paired seeds; 10 recommended. The matched-pairs sign test and matched-pairs rank-biserial effect size are appropriate at this N.\\n   D.5 CPU-only scope; results are not generalized to GPU-fused backends.\\n   D.6 Design-only deliverable; no execution claimed. Specifies procedures a future executor could implement mechanically.\\n\\n12. Protocol placeholders to be confirmed at execution time.\\n   P-1 Current TorchOpt alias for MetaAdam (for example torchopt.MetaAdam or torchopt.optim.meta.MetaAdam).\\n   P-2 Supported kwarg set of torchviz.make_dot in the executor's installed version; canonical call is torchviz.make_dot(loss, params=dict(model.named_parameters())).\\n   P-3 Parser choice for node and edge extraction (for example pydot from dot.source()).\\n   P-4 Op-type label definition if make_dot strips op attributes under chosen kwargs; default rule documented in 5.1.\\n   P-5 Eligibility rule when the immediate-successor heuristic fails; default is to exclude the run.\\n   P-6 String-match rules for AccumulateGrad and param.grad consumer identification.\\n   P-7 Default bucketing for ambiguous per-block nodes; cheapest-fit and flagged.\\n   P-8 Final seed count; minimum 5, recommended 10.\\n\\nEnd of design. Awaiting reviewer feedback on metric definitions, controls, placeholders, or the qualitative walkthrough table format before any execution attempt.\",\"evidence\":\"(1) Workspace inspection: ls -laR /workspace returns a single 0600 file task.json (466 B); no other files exist, including no track.md. The only reference to track.md is the string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" inside task.json.documents. (2) TorchOpt / TorchViz / torch local availability: find up to depth 7 returned zero directory matches for torchopt / torchviz / torch; Python 3.12.3 and pip 24.0 are present but import torch, import torchviz, import torchopt all fail with ModuleNotFoundError; no install attempted, in line with the no-network rule. (3) Researcher resolutions (a) through (d) restated verbatim in Section 10 so a reviewer can audit them, per explicit instruction. (4) No TorchOpt or TorchViz internals are fabricated beyond the API surface required by the design; every detail that cannot be stated with confidence is labeled with a protocol placeholder P-1 through P-8. (5) No execution is claimed; the design specifies procedures at a level a future executor could implement mechanically. (6) Format note: in this turn the arrays for assumptions and uncertainties are written as single-line item lists to match the only format that has validated so far in this session.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Researcher assumptions (a) through (d) recorded verbatim in Section 10 are taken as explicit assumptions of this design.</item>\",\"$text\":\"D.1 Four conditions (C0=torch.optim.Adam, C1=torchopt.Adam, C2=torchopt.MetaAdam, C_ped=unfused re-implementation) are the complete scope; no other fused-Adam variant is in scope.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.2 weight_decay=0.0 locked across all conditions to control B5 reporting.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.3 make_dot op labels assumed stable enough for the per-block bucketing; ambiguity handled by P-7.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.4 At least 5 paired seeds (10 recommended); statistical claims limited to matched-pairs sign test and matched-pairs rank-biserial effect size.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.5 CPU-only scope; results not generalized to GPU-fused backends.</item>\"},\"$text\":\"D.6 Design-only deliverable; no execution claimed; protocol placeholders P-1 through P-8 flagged for executor-time confirmation.</item>\"}],\"uncertainties\":[\"Final seed count within the 5-10 recommended band [P-8].\",\"Per-block bucketing default-overlap behavior when a node matches multiple blocks [P-7].\",\"String-match rules for AccumulateGrad and param.grad consumer identification [P-6].\",\"Heuristic for the param + update successor when multiple immediate successors exist [P-5].\",\"Whether op-type labels survive make_dot under the locked kwarg shape [P-4].\",\"Parser used to extract node and edge counts from the rendered dot [P-3].\",{\"item\":\"Current TorchOpt alias for the differentiable fused-meta Adam entry point [P-1].</item>\",\"$text\":\"Exact supported kwarg set of torchviz.make_dot in the executor's installed version [P-2], and whether params= triggers leaf-parameter edge rendering.</item>\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-7ee49406-1dad-4540-8959-87d0394f0f27", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 38}
